#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205011} :activity/id #uuid "f13da08f-0333-5cf7-8f52-a79e6bf0ebd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048550 :topic/id #uuid "a9165535-d837-58b0-9784-39a400db9d86" :topic/math-academy-id 5869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A$. It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11} & x_{12} \\\\ x_{21} & x_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x_{11}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{12}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x_{21}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{22}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x_{22}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} & -\\frac{x_{21}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{x_{12}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} & \\frac{x_{11}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1)/(det(X))\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= 3 \\cdot (-2) - 1 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-6 - 5 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= -\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nwould change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ace4da9-e2b2-56f1-b064-78e25344a30e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "51d89ff0-2e51-5813-be29-4f290760f1f7" :step/math-academy-id 450917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203857 :question/id #uuid "ca1002c8-c615-5f7b-8876-82cf87c8864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-17349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c676a83b-f3d4-579b-ab54-de2104552e8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050484}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204572}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function With Respect to a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5682316e-09ab-5709-825d-abbce84fe179" :step/math-academy-id 450918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left(\\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} = y_i x_j\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "957b6db8-152d-50d1-9685-1b280888ce3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12388 :tutorial/title "Component-Wise Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "40001fda-db52-5314-a824-eba6f4cc9e69" :step/math-academy-id 450921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204080 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203871 :question/id #uuid "5aa67862-6f7a-57ac-9473-d039fc4bfec4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35dc17b6-1c82-55ec-9657-20f2850794e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204584}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function Component-Wise"} :step/id #uuid "ad3cf801-8dd4-5f9a-b7ca-588e439c66cc" :step/math-academy-id 450922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A)$, defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\n, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n$∇_{y}L|= ∇_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}y^{T}y); = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot ∇_{y}(y^{T}y); = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2y; = y$.\nTherefore, we have\n$\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k$.\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n$y_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i$,\nwe have\n${x_{j},\\begin{vmatrix}i = k, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂L}{∂a_{ij}} &= ∑_(k = 1)^(2)\\frac{∂L}{∂y_{k}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{k}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂L}{∂y_{i}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i}x_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a8a781f-98de-5bf4-8f02-80c10f3211b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12479 :tutorial/title "Differentiation Using the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "da5331f6-b68e-5fed-80d1-95b4e0971765" :step/math-academy-id 450925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204081 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203866 :question/id #uuid "429a6a34-2fe5-5c09-9b74-e4b650209007" :question/math-academy-id "e-17870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7b2139-06eb-5584-a267-d9fbc9b5d041" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204585}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Calculate Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "0dbe27ec-63c8-5d16-a0de-f9deb9e9f3e8" :step/math-academy-id 450926}] :activity/title "Matrix Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204182}]}
